<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castillo de San Marcos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a>–<a href="/years/1695">1695</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Castillo de San Marcos is a Spanish masonry fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St. Augustine, Florida</a>, built between 1672 and 1695 from coquina, a local shell stone. It replaced a series of wooden forts and withstood English sieges in 1702 and 1740, which burned the town but never took the fort. The oldest masonry fort in the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, it is now a national monument.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castillo-de-san-marcos.jpg" alt="Castillo de San Marcos" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schwerdf · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Castillo_de_San_Marcos,_St._Augustine,_FL_2025-05-20.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>St. Augustine was founded in 1565 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> on the site of a Native American village called Seloy. After Sir Francis Drake attacked it in 1586, nine wooden forts were built over the next 80 years. When the English privateer Robert Searles burned the town in 1668, Mariana, Queen Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, approved a masonry fort, and Governor Francisco de la Guerra y de la Vega ordered it. The design was by the engineer Ignacio Daza, and work began on 2 October 1672 under Governor Manuel de Cendoya. Native Americans from the nearby missions did most of the labour, with skilled workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and African slaves owned by the Spanish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Castillo is a star fort of coquina quarried at the King's Quarry on Anastasia Island and ferried across Matanzas Bay. It has four bastions, San Pedro, San Agustín, San Carlos and San Pablo, with a ravelin guarding the sally port, a glacis on the two landward sides and a moat that could be flooded with seawater. The coquina absorbed cannonballs, which sank into the walls instead of shattering them. From 1738 the engineer Pedro Ruiz de Olano rebuilt the interior with vaulted ceilings, raising the outer wall from 26 to 33 feet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The fort was a refuge for slaves escaping from Carolina, whom the Spanish freed if they converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, which led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a>, the first free Black settlement in the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Possession changed five times, always peacefully. As Fort St. Mark under the British it held Christopher Gadsden; as Fort Marion under the United States it became a military prison. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osceola">Osceola</a> was held there in 1837, and that November Coacoochee and nineteen other Seminoles escaped. From 1875 western prisoners were held there under Richard Henry Pratt, whose experience led to the Carlisle Indian Industrial School, and from 1886 to 1887 about 491 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apaches</a>, at least 24 of whom died.</p><p>The fort became a national monument in 1924, passed to the National Park Service in 1933 and took back its Spanish name in 1942. In 1964 its Freedom Tree was a gathering place for civil rights demonstrators.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castillo de San Marcos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The stone <b>Castillo de San Marcos</b>, begun in 1672, resisted English sieges in 1702 and 1740, and Pensacola was founded in 1698.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a></li></ul></section>
